To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function. The `exit()` function causes immediate termination of the program, and any buffered output is flushed and any open files are closed.

Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates the use of `exit()` function:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");

    // Terminate the program using exit() function
    exit(0);

    // Code after exit() will not be executed
    printf("This line will not be printed.\n");

    return 0;
}
```

In the above example, the `exit()` function is called after printing a message to the console. Any code after the `exit()` function will not be executed. The `0` argument passed to the `exit()` function represents the exit status of the program.
